.TH "LMM" "1" "Oct 2026" "lmm 2.2.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
lmm-service - Run the web UI in the background as a systemd user service


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlmm service [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Run 'lmm serve' in the background as the systemd user service
lmm-serve.service, so the web UI is there whenever you are logged in.

.PP
If you installed lmm from a package (deb, rpm, apk or the AUR), the unit is
already installed, turned off; turn it on with:
  systemctl --user enable --now lmm-serve

.PP
\&'lmm service install' is for a tarball, 'go install' or 'make install':
it writes the unit to ~/.config/systemd/user, pointing at this lmm binary.

.PP
The service starts when you log in. To start it at boot without a login,
run 'loginctl enable-linger $USER' once; lmm never runs it for you.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for service


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lmm or ~/.config/lmm)

.PP
\fB--data\fP=""
	data directory (default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/lmm or ~/.local/share/lmm)

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--game\fP=""
	game ID to operate on

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output JSON instead of text; mutating commands print their result, --dry-run prints the plan; never prompts

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP="off"
	diagnostic log level written to stderr (off, error, warn, info, debug)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable colored output (NO_COLOR env is also honored)

.PP
\fB--no-hooks\fP[=false]
	disable all hooks

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


